1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A functional dependency X -> Y says: whenever two rows agree on every attribute of X, they must also agree on every attribute of Y.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Here the two rows agree on A, so the dependency A -> B forces them to agree on B as well. Agreeing on B would not have forced anything about A.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
One printed table can satisfy A -> B by accident. The dependency is the claim that no legal instance can ever break it.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So a dependency is a constraint you are given, not a pattern you discover. Data can refute an FD; data can never establish one.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Reflexivity: if every attribute of Y already sits inside X, then X -> Y holds with no help from the dependency set.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Augmentation: from X -> Y you may add the same attribute set Z to both sides, getting XZ -> YZ.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Transitivity: X -> Y together with Y -> Z gives X -> Z. Chaining is how a dependency set implies dependencies nobody wrote down.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Three useful shortcuts follow: union, decomposition and pseudotransitivity. Each one is proved from the three axioms, so using it is never a leap.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Start the closure of AB with AB itself, on R(A,B,C,D,E) with AB -> C, BC -> D and C -> E. This is the schema from GATE CSE 2022.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Pass one: AB -> C applies, because AB is already present. The closure grows to ABC.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Pass two: BC -> D now fires, because both B and C are present. The closure is ABCD.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Pass three: C -> E fires. The closure is ABCDE, all five attributes, so AB is a superkey.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
A superkey closes to all five attributes. A candidate key is a superkey that contains no smaller superkey.

14
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,000
All 32 subsets of the five attributes are tested. The ones that close to ABCDE are exactly the ones that contain both A and B.

15
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Exactly one subset is minimal: AB. So the schema has one candidate key, and 2 to the power 3, that is 8, superkeys.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
E on its own is not a key: its closure is just E. C on its own gives CE. Neither reaches all five attributes.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
First normal form asks only that every cell hold one atomic value. GATE 2018 Schema I stores a set of courses in one cell, so it is not even in 1NF.

18
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Second normal form forbids a non-prime attribute from depending on part of a candidate key. Schema IV has courseid -> credit, with key {rollno, courseid}.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,000
credit is non-prime and courseid is a proper subset of the key, so Schema IV is in 1NF only. That is the GATE 2018 answer for that schema.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Schema III has marks -> grade. marks is not part of any key at all, so this is a transitive dependency, not a partial one: Schema III reaches 2NF and stops.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Third normal form adds one escape clause to BCNF: a dependency X -> A is allowed even when X is not a superkey, provided A is a prime attribute.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Schema II: rollno, courseid -> email, and email -> rollno. The candidate keys are {rollno, courseid} and {email, courseid}, so rollno is prime.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
So Schema II survives 3NF and fails BCNF on the same dependency. The exemption is the only thing keeping it at 3NF.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
That is the answer to GATE 2018 Q42: Schema II is the one in 3NF but not BCNF. Schema III stops at 2NF and Schema IV at 1NF.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
BCNF deletes the exemption. Every non-trivial dependency must have a superkey on the left, with no exception for prime attributes.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
GATE 2004 studentPerformance(Name, CourseNo, RollNo, Grade) has keys {Name, CourseNo} and {RollNo, CourseNo}. So Name, CourseNo and RollNo are prime, and Grade is not.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Name -> RollNo has a non-superkey on the left but a prime right side, so 3NF allows it and BCNF does not. The schema is in 3NF only.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
To reach BCNF, split on the violating dependency: one fragment holds its two sides, the other holds the left side plus everything left over. Both fragments are then re-tested.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
GATE 2001: R(A,B,C,D) with A -> B and C -> D, decomposed into AB and CD. The two fragments share no attribute at all.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Take a two-row instance, project it onto ABC and onto AD, then join the two results. The join has four rows, not two.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Every original row survives, and two rows that never existed appear. That is what lossy means: r is a proper subset of the join, so information was invented.

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The two-fragment test: the join is lossless exactly when the shared attributes determine all of one fragment. Here the shared set is empty, so the test fails.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
A dependency is preserved when its left and right sides both fit inside one fragment, or when the fragments together still imply it.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
GATE 2001: R(A,B,C,D) with AB -> C and C -> AD, split into ABC and CD. AB -> C fits in ABC, and C -> A does too, so everything survives.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
But ABC is not in BCNF: C -> A has a non-superkey on the left. The model enumerated all 14 partitions of ABCD, and none is BCNF, lossless and dependency preserving at once.

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
So the three goals conflict. A 3NF decomposition always exists that is lossless and dependency preserving; a BCNF one need not. That trade-off is the examinable fact.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Step one: compute the closure of every candidate left side, and from the closures read off all candidate keys. Step two: mark the prime attributes.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Step three: reduce the set to a minimal cover, then ask of each dependency: is the left side a superkey, and is the right side prime. Those two answers place the schema on the ladder.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Step four: for a proposed decomposition, run the chase for losslessness and the projection test for preservation. Both are mechanical and both must pass.

40
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Two facts survive every variation: 3NF synthesis always gives a lossless, dependency preserving decomposition; BCNF cannot always be reached without giving one of them up.

